Commentary of Ibn Sa'di (Taysir al-Karim al-Rahman) on surah 20, verse 131:

{131} That is: Do not extend {your eyes} in admiration, nor repeatedly gaze approvingly at the conditions of this world and at those who enjoy its delights: its delicious foods and drinks, its luxurious clothing, its adorned houses, and its beautified women. For all of that is {the adornment of the worldly life} (zahrat al-hayat al-dunya); the souls of the deluded delight in it, and the eyes of those who turn away are captivated by it in admiration, and {the wrongdoing people} enjoy it while disregarding the Hereafter. Then it passes quickly and all of it comes to an end, and it destroys those who love it and are infatuated with it, so they will regret when regret will not benefit them, and they will know what they have become when they come on the Day of Resurrection. Allah made it only a trial and a test, to know who will stop at it and be deceived by it, and who is best in deed. As Allah the Exalted said: {Indeed, We have made what is on the earth an adornment for it, that We may test them as to which of them is best in deed. And indeed, We will make what is upon it a barren wasteland.}

{And the provision of your Lord}: the immediate (portion) of knowledge, faith, and the realities of righteous deeds, and the deferred (portion) of enduring bliss and a sound life in the proximity of the Merciful Lord. {Is better}: than what We have provided to the pairs (of them) in its essence and attributes, {and more enduring}: because it does not cease; its fruit is perpetual and its shade (is perpetual); as Allah the Exalted said: {But you prefer the worldly life. Yet the Hereafter is better and more enduring.}

In this verse there is an indication that when the servant sees in himself an aspiration toward the adornment of this world and an inclination toward it, he should remind himself of what lies ahead from the provision of his Lord, and weigh one against the other.
